contains only task.json (design-only), no BAdam/pipeline/transformer code, no preinstalled ML stack, network disabled, no GPU. No experiment run and no result claimed.", "assumptions": ["A1: BAdam = bounded adaptive gradient method with l2-ball bound rho_t, beta1=0.9/beta2=0.999/eps=1e-8, warmup 10%, global bound; knobs rho_max/warmup/per-module-vs-global", "A2: primary perturbation = net W(T)-W(0); per-step updates secondary", "A3: effective rank = spectral entropy (primary) + participation ratio (cross-check)", "A4: comparable = relative diff within 10-20% and/or overlapping seed CIs (both stated)", "A5: ~10-50M decoder-only transformer, CLM finetuning on small fixed corpus, 1k-5k steps, LR scale-matched, 3-5 seeds identical init/data/shuffle", "Heavy-tail operational threshold alpha <= 2", "Default config 6 layers d=384 6 heads FFN 1152 vocab 32k; WikiText-2 subset; ~15-20M params"], "uncertainties": ["Exact BAdam update/bound/beta/epsilon details are not in the repo; if the intended variant differs only the optimizer-implementation section changes", "Whether pretrained vs random init is available affects absolute spectra but not the paired comparison", "Corpus availability offline: WikiText-2 subset assumed, synthetic token stream as fallback", "Small seed count (3-5) limits CI reliability; noted small-n caveat", "Heavy-tail threshold alpha<=2 is a chosen operational default, tunable"], "answer": "# Experiment Design: Does BAdam produce a learning perturbation with a heavy-tailed singular-value distribution and an effective rank comparable to Adam across transformer modules during finetuning?\n\n## 0. Scope and status\nDesign-only task. No experiment has been run; no result is claimed. This document specifies a reproducible protocol. The environment inspected (`/workspace`, only `task.json`) contains no BAdam code, no finetuning pipeline, and no preinstalled ML stack, so every component is defined here.\n\n## 1. Pinned assumptions (labeled, not verified facts)\n- **A1 \u2014 BAdam identity.** BAdam = the **bounded adaptive gradient method** (not a momentum variant). Update rule (per parameter element i), with per-step update norm bounded by a budget:\n  - Preconditioned step: `u_t = m_t / (sqrt(v_t) + \u03b5)` where `m_t = \u03b21 m_{t-1} + (1-\u03b21) g_t`, `v_t = \u03b22 v_{t-1} + (1-\u03b22) g_t^2` (Adam-style first/second moments).\n  - **Bounding (the BAdam-specific mechanism):** project the full-batch update onto an \u21132 ball of radius `\u03c1_t`: `\u0394\u03b8_t = \u03c1_t \u00b7 u_t / max(\u2016u_t\u2016\u2082, \u03c1_t)`.\n  - **Bound/beta schedule (default):** `\u03c1_t = \u03c1_max \u00b7 min(1, t/warmup)` with `\u03c1_max` set so the typical per-step update norm under Adam \u2248 `\u2016\u0394\u03b8_t\u2016\u2082` (scale-matched, see A5); default `warmup = 10%` of steps; `\u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999, \u03b5=1e-8` (match Adam to isolate the bounding effect).\n  - **BAdam-specific knobs:** `\u03c1_max` (bound radius), `warmup`, and optionally a **per-module** vs **global** bound. Default = global bound.\n  - *Transfer note (feasibility \u00a75):* if the intended BAdam is a different bounded-adaptive variant, only \u00a73.1 (optimizer implementation) changes; the analysis pipeline is optimizer-agnostic.\n- **A2 \u2014 Learning perturbation (primary):** `\u0394W_m = W_m(T) \u2212 W_m(0)`, the net weight change of module `m` over finetuning. **Secondary sanity view:** per-step update matrices `U_m(t) = W_m(t) \u2212 W_m(t-1)` (and their sum, which equals `\u0394W_m`), retained to confirm the primary object is well-defined and stable.\n- **A3 \u2014 Effective rank (primary):** spectral entropy `r_eff = exp(\u2212\u03a3_i p_i ln p_i)`, `p_i = \u03c3_i / \u03a3_j \u03c3_j`. **Robustness cross-check:** participation ratio `r_eff^PR = (\u03a3 \u03c3_i)\u00b2 / \u03a3 \u03c3_i\u00b2`.\n- **A4 \u2014 \"Comparable\":** two stated criteria, both reported: (i) **relative difference** `|r_eff^BAdam \u2212 r_eff^Adam| / r_eff^Adam` within **10\u201320%**; (ii) **overlapping seed-level CIs** (95% bootstrap CIs across seeds). A module is \"comparable\" if it satisfies both; the module is flagged \"not comparable\" if it fails either.\n- **A5 \u2014 Default setup:** small **decoder-only transformer (~10\u201350 M params)**; **causal-LM finetuning** on a **small fixed corpus**; **~1k\u20135k steps**; **LR scale-matched** between optimizers (critical control, \u00a73.2); **3\u20135 seeds** per optimizer with **identical init / data order / shuffle seed**, only the optimizer differing.\n\n## 2. Objective (answerable claims)\n- **Claim H (heavy-tailed):** Is the singular-value tail of `\u0394W_m` under BAdam heavy-tailed (power-law, low tail index) AND meaningfully heavier-tailed than under Adam, per module?\n- **Claim R (rank-comparable):** Is `r_eff(\u0394W_m)` under BAdam comparable to Adam per module under criteria A4?\n- The overall question is answered by the joint pattern across modules (both claims).\n\n## 3. Experimental setup\n### 3.1 Model, data, task (A5)\n- **Model:** decoder-only transformer, target ~10\u201350 M params. Concrete default: **6 layers, hidden `d=384`, 6 heads (`d_head=64`), FFN dim `1152`, vocab 32k** (\u224815\u201320 M params). Configurable to 8 layers / `d=512` for the upper bound.\n- **Data:** a single fixed corpus, e.g. a **subset of WikiText-2** (~10\u201350 M tokens), tokenized once; identical split/order across all runs. (If no corpus is downloadable offline, substitute a fixed synthetic token stream of matched length/entropy \u2014 noted as a fallback.)\n- **Task:** standard causal-LM next-token finetuning from a pretrained-or-random init (random init is acceptable and reduces confounds; if pretrained weights are available, use the same checkpoint for all runs).\n\n### 3.2 Modules and controls\n**Primary 2D modules (the \"various transformer modules\"):**\n- Attention per layer: `W_Q`, `W_K`, `W_V`, `W_O` (4/layer).\n- FFN per layer: `W_gate`/`W_up` (up-projection) and `W_down` (down-projection) (2/layer).\n- **Excluded / handled separately (stated reason):** token **embedding** and **LM-head** are large but are analyzed as a separate rank-degenerate block (they are often tied and behave differently); **LayerNorms** (and biases) are 1-D (rank-1) so their SVD is degenerate \u2014 they are reported only as a scalar-norm sanity check, not in the heavy-tail/effective-rank analysis.\n- **Controls (required):**\n  1. **LR / scale-matching control (A5):** choose BAdam `\u03c1_max` and/or LR so that the *typical per-step update norm* `median_t \u2016\u0394\u03b8_t\u2016\u2082` matches Adam's within ~10% (verify empirically in a short calibration run). This isolates the bounding mechanism from a raw step-size effect.\n  2. **Frozen / no-update baseline:** a run with `lr=0` (weights frozen) \u2192 `\u0394W=0`, `r_eff` undefined/0. Confirms the measured perturbation is optimizer-driven (nonzero only when the optimizer updates) and gives a null reference for the spectrum.\n  3. **Optimizer-only difference:** Adam and BAdam share init, data, shuffle, LR scale, batch size, and all hyperparameters except the optimizer body.\n\n### 3.3 Run matrix\n- Optimizers: {Adam, BAdam}. Seeds: **3\u20135** (default 5). Controls: {frozen baseline \u00d71}.\n- Total primary runs = 2 optimizers \u00d7 5 seeds = **10 finetuning runs** (+1 frozen). Each run: ~1k\u20135k steps, batch 8\u201316.\n- Identical init seed, data order, and shuffle seed per paired (Adam, BAdam) run.\n\n## 4. Analysis plan\nFor each module `m`, each seed `s`, each optimizer `o`:\n1. **Perturbation:** `\u0394W_{m,s,o} = W_{m,s,o}(T) \u2212 W_{m,s,o}(0)`. Secondary: per-step stack `U_{m,s,o}(t)`, verify `\u03a3_t U(t) \u2248 \u0394W`.\n2. **SVD:** full singular values `\u03c3_1\u2265\u2026\u2265\u03c3_r`, `r = min(rows, cols)`.\n3. **Heavy-tail test:**\n   - **Tail range selection:** fit the upper tail over a principled range. Sort `\u03c3`; use the values above the `p`-th percentile with `p` chosen as the smallest x that (a) has \u226530 points in the tail and (b) minimizes the Kolmogorov\u2013Smirnov distance to the best-fit power law (standard Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman style range selection). Report the chosen range.\n   - **Tail index:** (i) **MLE power-law** fit `P(\u03c3)\u221d\u03c3^{-\u03b1}` on the chosen tail \u2192 `\u03b1` with SE; (ii) **Hill estimator** on the top-k values as a cross-check; (iii) **log-log linear slope** as a sanity view.\n   - **Goodness-of-fit vs exponential:** fit an exponential decay `P(\u03c3)\u221de^{-\u03bb\u03c3}` to the same range; compare via **likelihood ratio / AIC** and a **KS or Anderson\u2013Darling test** of each model against the empirical tail. \"Heavy-tailed\" = power law fits significantly better than exponential **and** `\u03b1` is low (default operational threshold `\u03b1 \u2272 2`, reported explicitly).\n   - **Per-module verdict:** report `\u03b1_BAdam`, `\u03b1_Adam`, and the GOF comparison; flag BAdam as \"heavier-tailed\" if its power-law fit is better and `\u03b1_BAdam < \u03b1_Adam` by a meaningful margin (report the margin).\n4. **Effective rank:**\n   - Primary `r_eff = exp(\u2212\u03a3 p_i ln p_i)`; cross-check `r_eff^PR = (\u03a3\u03c3)\u00b2/\u03a3\u03c3\u00b2`.\n   - **Per-seed** values \u2192 pooled **95% bootstrap CI** (resample seeds, e.g., 10k resamples; with only 3\u20135 seeds use a parametric/percentile CI and note the small-n caveat).\n   - **Comparability (A4):** compute relative difference and CI overlap for each module; report both.\n5. **Reporting:** per-module, per-seed tables + aggregate plots: (i) sorted-\u03c3 curves on log-log (Adam vs BAdam, per module, seed-fan), (ii) tail fits with `\u03b1` and GOF, (iii) `r_eff` box/CI plots per module and optimizer.\n6. **Conclusion logic:** answer H and R per module, then aggregate across modules to answer the overall question (e.g., \"BAdam's perturbation is heavy-tailed and rank-comparable to Adam in X of Y modules; exceptions: \u2026\").\n\n## 5. Feasibility\n- **Compute:** CPU-only, no GPU required at ~10\u201350 M params. A ~15\u201320 M model at batch 8\u201316, seq 128, on a modern multi-core CPU (224 cores available) does roughly **~0.5\u20133 s/step** (single-process torch + multithreaded BLAS). 5k steps \u2248 **~1\u20133 h per run** single-process; 10 runs \u2248 **~10\u201330 h single-process**, reducible to **~1\u20132 h with 8\u201316 parallel processes** (seeds are embarrassingly parallel).\n- **Feasible-check budget:** a **pilot** of 1 seed \u00d7 {Adam, BAdam} \u00d7 ~500 steps first, to (a) confirm the scale-matching control, (b) sanity-check SVD/estimator code on real `\u0394W`, and (c) measure wall-time to set the full step count.\n- **If budget is tight, reduce in this order:** (1) seeds 5\u21923; (2) steps 5k\u21921k; (3) model ~20 M\u2192~10 M (fewer layers); (4) modules: keep Q/K/V/O + FFN down, drop gate/up duplication; (5) drop per-step secondary view. Heavy-tail + effective-rank estimators are cheap (SVD on \u22641k\u00d71k matrices) and are not the bottleneck.\n- **BAdam transfer:** if the intended BAdam is a different bounded-adaptive variant, only \u00a73.1's optimizer implementation (bound rule / \u03b2 / \u03b5 / knobs) changes; model, data, run matrix, and \u00a74 analysis are unchanged.\n\n## 6. Assumptions vs. requirements\n- **Fixed by task/researcher (requirements):** the three quantities' meanings as pinned (A2, A3, A4), BAdam = bounded adaptive gradient (A1), the default setup (A5), paired seeds, required controls (LR/scale-match + frozen baseline), and the four deliverable sections (a\u2013d) above.\n- **My labeled assumptions (not verified):** the specific BAdam update/bound/beta/\u03b5 details in A1 (no source in repo); the exact model config, corpus, step/LR/batch numbers, seed count, and the `\u03b1 \u2272 2` heavy-tail threshold are defaults chosen within the approved ranges and are tunable without changing the design."}", "